+# CONTRACT — Factory Reset
+
+The implementation lives in `src/index.ts` and exports `Machine`, `solveToggle`, and
+`solveCounter`. Both solvers reduce the puzzle to a linear system (GF(2) for toggle,
+GF(p) for counter), solve it via Gaussian elimination, and enumerate the solution
+space (null-space combinations) to find the optimal solution under the tie-break rules.
+
+## Part 1 — `solveToggle(machine): number[] | null`
+
+System over GF(2): `A x = target`, where column `j` of `A` is the set of lights button
+`j` toggles, `x_j ∈ {0,1}` is whether button `j` is pressed.
+
+- [ ] Returns ascending-sorted array of pressed button indices.
+      → test: result is sorted, each index in `[0, M)`.
+- [ ] Pressing exactly the returned buttons reproduces `target`.
+      → test: simulate toggles, compare to target (the fundamental correctness check).
+- [ ] Returns the solution with the FEWEST presses.
+      → test: brute-force min cardinality on small instances equals `result.length`.
+- [ ] On ties in count, returns the lexicographically smallest sorted index list.
+      → test: hand-crafted instance with two minimal solutions; smaller list returned.
+- [ ] Returns `null` when no subset of buttons yields `target`.
+      → test: the TASK.md example `[T,T,F,T]` returns `null`; other unsolvable cases.
+- [ ] `target` all-false → returns `[]` (press nothing, weight 0 is optimal).
+      → test: returns `[]`.
+- [ ] A button that toggles no lights (empty list) is never pressed.
+      → test: such buttons never appear in the result.
+- [ ] Duplicate light indices within one button cancel (parity per light).
+      → test: button `[0,0]` behaves as toggling nothing.
+- [ ] Out-of-range / negative light indices are ignored, never crash.
+      → test: button referencing index ≥ N does not appear to affect any light.
+- [ ] Handles 40 buttons / 40 lights with modest null-space dimension in < 5 s.
+      → test: timed large instance completes and is correct.
+- [ ] Does not mutate the input `machine`.
+      → test: deep-equal input before/after.
+
+## Part 2 — `solveCounter(lights, buttons, modulus): number[] | null`
+
+System over GF(p), p = `modulus` (prime): `A x ≡ lights (mod p)`, where column `j`
+counts how many of each counter button `j` increments, `x_j ∈ {0..p-1}` = press count.
+
+- [ ] Returns array of length M, each entry in `[0, modulus)`.
+      → test: length and range checks.
+- [ ] Applying the presses (mod p) reproduces `lights`.
+      → test: simulate increments mod p, compare to `lights` (fundamental check).
+- [ ] Returns solution with MINIMUM total presses (sum of entries).
+      → test: brute-force min sum on small instances equals `sum(result)`.
+- [ ] On ties in total, returns the lexicographically smallest result array.
+      → test: hand-crafted tie; element-wise smallest array returned.
+- [ ] Returns `null` when the system is inconsistent.
+      → test: unsolvable instance returns `null`.
+- [ ] `lights` all-zero → returns all-zero array.
+      → test: returns array of zeros.
+- [ ] A button affecting no counters is pressed 0 times.
+      → test: its entry is 0.
+- [ ] Duplicate counter indices within a button add up (per-press increment > 1).
+      → test: button `[0,0]` increments counter 0 by 2 per press.
+- [ ] Requires multiple presses of a button when needed (entry can be > 1).
+      → test: instance whose only solution needs an entry ≥ 2.
+- [ ] Handles up to 40 buttons / 40 counters / modulus ≤ 97 with small free-dim in < 5 s.
+      → test: timed instance completes and is correct.
+- [ ] Does not mutate inputs.
+      → test: inputs unchanged.
+
+## Done
+
+`npm run build` clean, `npm run lint` clean, `npm test` all green, every box above checked.
